    <div class="gp-sec" aria-label="The mechanism"><div class="gp-wrap"><h2>Your Body Still Knows How To Make It. It's Just Stopped Getting The Inputs.</h2><p>Here's what my doctor explained that changed how I saw the whole thing: for most men, the machinery still works. Your body hasn't lost the <em>ability</em> to produce testosterone. It's producing less because three inputs have quietly degraded:</p><ol class="gp-list"><li><b>Cortisol.</b> The stress hormone sits on the same production pathway. High cortisol suppresses output &mdash; and modern life keeps it high all day.</li><li><b>Recovery.</b> Most production happens during deep sleep. Lighter sleep after 35 means a smaller production window every single night.</li><li><b>Nutrient gaps.</b> The specific compounds that support the pathway &mdash; the ones men in Southeast Asia and the Andes have used for centuries &mdash; are simply absent from a Western diet.</li></ol><div class="gp-pull"><p>&ldquo;You haven't lost the machinery. You've lost the inputs.&rdquo;</p></div><p>You can't out-train that. I tried, and it made things worse. <mark>But you can feed the pathway</mark> &mdash; daily, consistently, for long enough that it compounds.</p><figure class="gp-fig"><img src="data:image/webp;base64," alt="Testosterone at 30 vs 45" loading="lazy" width="900" height="900"><figcaption>Roughly 1% a year from the mid-30s &mdash; gradual enough that most men never connect the dots.</figcaption></figure></div>
